# invariant — a universal law is enforced by a gate that quantifies over its
# set, not by a list of the sites that were fixed when it was written.
#
# The surface is every `## H-n` heading in INVARIANTS.md. `proven` means a named
# test *derives* the set it checks — from the cobra tree, from the source, from
# the spec, from the test corpus — so a site added tomorrow is covered without
# anybody remembering to add it. A long "Enforced by:" list is not proof of
# that; it is proof of the opposite, because the list is where the scope came
# from.
#
# H-13 is the one law this repository already enforced that way, and it is the
# one that has never produced a repeat defect.
#
# The ceiling is the number of laws still enforced by enumeration. It may fall
# without ceremony and may only rise by editing this line on purpose.
#ceiling 5

# --- proven: a gate derives the set ------------------------------------------
H-1 = proven: TestRetrySurfaceIsClosed
H-2 = proven: TestConfirmSurfaceIsClosed
H-7 = proven: TestDecodeSurfaceIsClosed
H-10 = proven: TestJSONContract
H-12 = proven: TestWriteBackSurfaceIsClosed
H-13 = proven: TestSpecResponseFieldsAreDecoded
H-14 = proven: TestLooksDegenerate_FlagsPoisonedDecode
H-16 = proven: TestMapRangeSurfaceIsClosed
H-17 = proven: TestTargetSurfaceIsClosed
H-19 = proven: TestAssertionFloor
H-20 = proven: TestClockSurfaceIsClosed
H-21 = proven: TestDomainDecodeSurfaceIsClosed
H-22 = proven: TestPositionalSurfaceIsClosed

# --- exempt: the law is about one site, so there is no set to quantify over ---
H-3 = exempt: there is exactly one vendored spec file, and make check-spec-drift diffs the whole of it byte for byte
H-4 = exempt: one write path, verified end to end; the law names a single sequence rather than a class of sites
H-5 = exempt: one write path — the backup precedes the write in the same function, and there is no second place it could fail to
H-6 = exempt: a property of the backup naming scheme itself, which exists in exactly one function

# --- debt: stated as a universal, enforced by an enumeration ------------------
H-8 = debt: twenty-six cited tests, all naming particular commands; nothing derives the set of commands that report an area
H-9 = debt: the law covers every lookup key hactl uses, and the citations enumerate the ones that were wrong once
H-11 = debt: the law covers every count hactl prints, and the citations name the counts that were found wrong rather than the set of counts
H-15 = debt: the law covers every detector, and one test is cited
H-18 = debt: reconciliation between two specific columns, stated as a rule about counts in general. The script family now shares the automation logbook path, so the two runs_24h columns agree; nothing derives the set of columns the rule covers
